course: dictionary
lesson: 1377
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The words around them, their context, tell you which meaning is needed. «The king fastened his red cape and walked to the throne.» A king does not fasten land, so cape here means a loose coat with no sleeves that hangs from the shoulders.
explain: «The ship sailed slowly round the cape, where the sea was rough and grey.» A ship sails on the sea, not in a coat, so this cape must be a piece of land that sticks out into the sea. The clues are ship and sea.
explain: Always check your choice. Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. Read the whole sentence before you decide, because the clues are often in the words that come after the hard word.
fact: A cape can be a loose coat with no sleeves that hangs from the shoulders, like a short cloak.
fact: A cape can also be a piece of land that sticks out into the sea; it is a headland.
fact: Superheroes, wizards and kings often wear capes in stories and dressing up.
fact: Ships sailing round capes often met storms, and Cape Horn and the Cape of Good Hope are famous examples.
fact: A rain cape is a coat that keeps cyclists dry.
exercise: «The superhero swung his cape as he flew over the city.» What is a cape here? | A small wooden boat | A kind of tall, thin bird | A piece of land that sticks out into the sea | *A loose coat with no sleeves that hangs from the shoulders
exercise: «Ships sailing round the cape often met storms.» What is a cape here? | A sail on a ship | *A piece of land that sticks out into the sea | A loose coat with no sleeves | A sudden gust of wind
exercise: Cyclists wear a rain cape to stay dry. What is a rain cape? | A map of the coast | A helmet with a visor | *A loose coat that keeps you dry in the rain | A bicycle wheel
test: «The bullfighter swept his cape, and the bull charged at it because it moved.» Which meaning of cape fits? | A rope tied to a boat | A cold, wet wind | A headland, a point of land in the sea | *A loose cloak, worn or waved like one
test: «The island lies near Cape Guardafui, the very tip of the Horn of Africa.» What does cape mean here? | A sort of tall tower | A loose coat worn by a king | *A piece of land that sticks out into the sea | Something to keep you dry in the rain
exam: «The old king fastened his cape and walked to the cape to watch the ships.» Which pair of meanings fits best? | Both mean a rain storm | Both mean a tall tower | *First a loose cloak, then a headland | First a headland, then a loose cloak
